  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nCurrent synoptic patterns of zonal flow to our north have allowed \nfor a return of seasonably warm temperatures into the 80s, along \nwith calm conditions and overall fair weather. A high pressure \ncenter located in the upper Great Plains has also given influence to \nour weather pattern, and will continue to progress to our north into \nthe Great Lakes region. The high will allow for light and variable \nwinds on Thursday, and southerly winds on Friday. We will continue \nto see seasonable temperatures, fair skies, and overall, pleasant \nweather for the rest of the workweek, and into the weekend.\n\nInto the weekend, a surface low pressure will begin to develop over \nthe upper Great Plains, and into southern Canada. Additionally, an \nupper-ridge and an upper-high pressure system will allow for \nsoutherly flow and warm-air advection for temperatures to remain in \nthe 80s. The pressure differences over the central part of the CONUS \nwill allow winds to increase this weekend with occasional gusts, \nespecially for areas across Northwestern Missouri. Our other story \nfor this weekend will be the eventual remnants of Tropical Storm \nIsaias, which as of Wednesday afternoon, is building just north of \nthe Yucatan Peninsula. This disturbance will progress northward and \nbe to our east by Saturday night, and continue to travel eastward on \nSunday as a result of shortwave development with respect to the jet \nstream beginning to dig southward. Not expecting any impacts from \nthe tropical depression, with the exception of increased cloud cover \nin our eastern counties.\n\nAs the weekend progresses, a deepening troughing feature is expected \nto begin to come in contact with a cut-off low off the coast of the \nPacific. In addition, an upper-low will develop over the California \ncoast. This will result in both temperature and moisture advection \ninto early next week. The trough is expected to come through the \nregion into the middle of next week, which will bring our next \nchances for more seasonable temperatures and rainfall. There is \nstill uncertainty of exact timing, and amount of rainfall, as \nensemble spread is much higher beginning on Tuesday.",
